        "uniques": ["Cannot enter ocean tiles", "Can move after attacking"],
        "civilopediaText": [
            {
                "text": "Dhow ply the Indian Ocean and Red Sea and have for centuries. The word 'dhow' has Arabic origins, but the ships were a vital line of trade between India, East Africa, and the Middle East. From ports such as Lamu in Kenya to Adulis in Aksum, Musa in Yemen, Gujarat in India, and eastwards, the sharp-prowed dhow with its lateen (triangular) sail carried incense, ivory, and tortoise from the African coast to exchange with precious metals and wine elsewhere. Until the 19th century, human beings were also part of this cargo. \nAs a general term, “dhow” also encompasses a wide body of similar kinds of ships, from the massive two-masted baghlah (a 30-meter ship with 30-40 crewmembers) that would undertake the longest voyages, to the smaller fishing dhow, crewed by a dozen or so men. The success of these various models created an opportunity for piracy, and many larger dhow formed the core of pirate fleets operating in and around the region."
            },
            { "separator": true }
        ],
        "upgradesTo": "Caravel",
