        "promotions": ["[Magarru] ability"],
        "civilopediaText": [
            {
                "text": "'Magarru' means chariot, a key weapon within the Neo-Assyrian army. Although horses had been in use in Mesopotamia since at least the Akkadian Empire, the lack of saddles, stirrups, and other equipment made them difficult to ride effectively in war. They were more easily managed when harnessed to chariots. These vehicles were small and light, possessing two wheels and carrying two passengers, a driver and an archer. Because horses are naturally averse to charging at lines of soldiers, they were saved until the enemy was about, or had begun, to flee – then the chariots were deployed to swiftly deliver the archer to his quarry. \nThe downside of chariots was that they were expensive to produce, and so using them on the field was a financial risk. But by the reign of Sargon II, in the eight century BCE, horse tack and horsemanship began to improve, and soon cavalry surpassed chariots as the army’s mobile unit."
            },
            { "separator": true }
        ],
        "attackSound": "arrow"
    },

    {
        "name": "Colonist",
        "replaces": "Settler",
        "uniqueTo": "Qart Hashdat",
        "unitType": "Civilian",
        "movement": 2,
        "cost": 106,
        "uniques": [
            "Founds a new city <by consuming this unit>",
            "Comment [Cities founded by this unit start with 1 extra population]",
            "Excess Food converted to Production when under construction",
            "Requires at least [2] population"
        ],
        "civilopediaText": [
            {
                "text": "Carthage began as a Phoenician colony, a small coastal outpost founded to facilitate and control trade across the Mediterranean. As it grew into a city and an independent center of power, Carthage used the same technique to expand its own influence and power, relying on colonies rather than land to fuel its empire. While historically this expansion has been characterized as aggressive and imperialistic (in large part due to hostile Roman accounts of Carthage’s history), more recent investigations have shown that the initial coalescence of power focused on taking control of, and strengthening, existing Phoenician settlements along the western coastlines, while direct seizure of territory occurred later on. During this later phase, colonists were sent to establish settlements around the Mediterranean and beyond to function as trade hubs, depots, and safe harbors for the Carthaginian fleet."
            },
            { "separator": true }
        ],
        "hurryCostModifier": 20
    },

    {
        "name": "Medjay",
