    {
        "name": "Yumi",
        "unitType": "Archery",
        "replaces": "Composite Bowman",
        "uniqueTo": "Heian Japan",
        "movement": 3,
        "strength": 7,
        "rangedStrength": 11,
        "cost": 75,
        "requiredTech": "Construction",
        "obsoleteTech": "Machinery",
        "promotions": ["[Yumi] ability"],
        "civilopediaText": [
            {
                "text": "A term for a weapon, not a person, yumi refers to the Japanese bow. Along with the iconic sword (in Heian’s time, the heavier tachi), the bow was one of the significant weapons of the samurai class. A yumi is smaller on the bottom, but extends upwards in a large arc. Its massive frame meant power, and its asymmetry meant that it could be used on horseback or kneeling. During Heian, bowyers experimented with fusing wood and bamboo to further increase the projectile power of the bow, making an already deadly weapon more devastating. The most famous Japanese archer of the time was Nasu no Yoichi, a samurai of the Minamoto clan who allegedly shot the Taira banner from their flagship in one shot, demonstrating the superiority of the Minamoto forces in the conflicts between the clans."
            },
            { "separator": true }
        ],
        "upgradesTo": "Crossbowman",
        "attackSound": "arrow"
