    {
        "name": "Yuthahathi",
        "replaces": "Horseman",
        "uniqueTo": "Khmer",
        "unitType": "Mounted",
        "movement": 4,
        "strength": 13,
        "cost": 75,
        "requiredTech": "Horseback Riding",
        "upgradesTo": "Knight",
        "obsoleteTech": "Chivalry",
        "uniques": [
            "Can move after attacking",
            "No defensive terrain bonus",
            "[-33]% Strength <vs cities> <when attacking>"
        ],
        "civilopediaText": [
            {
                "text": "'Yuthahati' is an archaic term, derived from spoken, rather than written, South Asian languages (compare 'hathi', meaning elephant, to the written Sanskrit or Pali 'gaja'). Still, it refers to the royal war elephants across the region. As seen in the carvings at Angkor, the king rides on his royal elephant above the masses of soldiers, literally and symbolically above his subjects. This is a fitting metaphor for royalty in Southeast Asia, but it also displays a tactical advantage. Elephants are not invulnerable to spears and swords, but they are hardier than smaller mounts. From their back, a warrior could fire over shields and the front ranks or duel a rival king with pikes. The symbolism continues in this clash of kings that occurs above the common fray, displayed where the entire battlefield could see its outcome. Elephants are also surprisingly quiet and capable of navigating muddy terrain where horses falter. They remained dominant in Southeast Asia even while horse archers were putting an end to the elephant’s dominance in drier climates."
            },
            { "separator": true }
        ],
        "hurryCostModifier": 20,
        "attackSound": "elephant"
    },

    {
        "name": "Purabhettarah",
        "replaces": "Horseman",
        "uniqueTo": "Maurya",
        "unitType": "Mounted",
        "movement": 3,
        "strength": 12,
        "cost": 75,
        "requiredTech": "Horseback Riding",
        "upgradesTo": "Knight",
        "obsoleteTech": "Chivalry",
        "uniques": [
            "Can move after attacking",
            "No defensive terrain bonus",
            "[+50]% Strength <vs cities> <when attacking>"
        ],
        "civilopediaText": [
            {
                "text": "Known as a 'city-breaker', the Mauryan war elephant was a battering ram – a living hulk of armored flesh trained to smash, ram, and stomp city walls. The advance of Vedic people across the subcontinent is most clearly associated with the chariot (the wheel being a core symbol of Vedic conquests), but the elephant also left a lasting impact. When Alexander attempted the invasion of India, his exhaustion finally caught up with him after battles with war elephants. The slightly later Mauryans fielded nearly ten thousand elephants in their wars, and elephants remained dominant against all but the best horsemen – their trumpeting would scatter horses and infantry. The Mongols won some brief advantages, turning their highly-trained and nimble horses into mobile archery platforms, but the use of elephants didn't wane until the adoption of cannons (even muskets were relatively ineffective against the behemoths)."
            },
            { "separator": true }
        ],
        "hurryCostModifier": 20,
        "attackSound": "elephant"
    },

    {
        "name": "Jaguar Hunter",
        "replaces": "Scout",
        "uniqueTo": "Ki'iche",
        "unitType": "Sword",
        "movement": 2,
        "strength": 6,
        "cost": 25,
        "uniques": [
            "May upgrade to [Archer] through ruins-like effects",
            "Can instantly construct a [Jaguar Trap] improvement <once>"
        ],
        "promotions": ["Ignore terrain cost"],
        "civilopediaText": [
            {
                "text": "The wolf, the bear, the lion, and the tiger are all symbols of nobility in the Old World, an effort to identify with beasts that seemed to rule the natural order. In the New World, that creature was the jaguar, the largest predator of the Mesoamerican jungles, renowned for its deadliness and its beauty. Jaguar-dressed warriors were a feature of the Aztec military, but Maya nobility also saw itself in that luxuriant orange fur. Like the jaguar, elite Maya warriors were adept at navigating dense jungles and relied on surprise and ambush over the rigid phalanx lines of ancient Old World armies. \nIn Maya mythology, jaguars represented hostile outside forces threatening civilization. They were mighty, but those who slayed them were mightier still. Yet the legendary jaguar slayers did not traditionally face the animals head-on. To overcome the physical disadvantages of humanity, hunters used tricks to outwit the jaguars. A key element of their toolkit was the jaguar trap, a deadfall set to drop a large boulder on approaching enemies. This trap was a powerful weapon against the jaguar and other foes, inflicting not only injury, but utter defeat."
            },
            { "separator": true }
        ],
        "hurryCostModifier": 19,
        "attackSound": "metalhit"
    },

    {
        "name": "Burning Arrow",
        "replaces": "Archer",
        "uniqueTo": "Mississipi",
        "unitType": "Archery",
        "movement": 2,
        "strength": 5,
        "rangedStrength": 7,
        "range": 2,
        "cost": 40,
