        "upgradesTo": "Composite Bowman",
        "obsoleteTech": "Construction",
        "uniques": ["[+50]% Strength <vs cities> <when attacking>"],
        "promotions": ["[Burning Arrows] ability"],
        "civilopediaText": [
            {
                "text": "Mississippian warfare typically utilized clubs and stone axes for melee weapons. Clubs were a point of pride and skill – the weapon of nobility. Battles appear to have been significant enough that Mississippians built fortifications around major settlements, although these defenses may have been designed to prevent war via intimidation rather than defend against actual siege. \nFor ranged attacks, thick bows held a draw of about fifty pounds (about half the force of an English longbow). Mississippian cities were constructed of wood and thatch. This meant that fire was a devastating weapon against them. Evidence from Cahokia shows that fire arrows were used offensively during raids – the resulting devastation and confusion only increased the shock value of such warfare."
            },
            { "separator": true }
        ],
        "attackSound": "arrow"
    },

    {
        "name": "Xshasan Immortal",
        "replaces": "Spearman",
        "uniqueTo": "Xshaca",
        "unitType": "Sword",
        "movement": 2,
        "strength": 11,
        "cost": 56,
        "requiredTech": "Bronze Working",
        "uniques": ["[+50]% Strength <vs [Mounted] units>"],
        "promotions": ["[Xshasan Immortal] ability"],
        "civilopediaText": [
            {
                "text": "The Persian Immortals became a legendary part of the Achaemenid army. However, there remains some confusion concerning the accuracy of the term 'immortal'. We know the name thanks to the Greek historian Herodotus, but his sources were limited, and he often preferred a good story to verified fact. \nAccording to Herodotus, the Immortals were not undying warriors, but their unit was intended to be eternal. As soon as a member of the company became unable to fight, he was immediately replaced by another, so that a unit was always at full strength. Descriptions of their exact composition vary but include leather-clad javelin throwers and heavily-armored cavalry. Their arms likely varied over time, region, and battle. Whatever they might have called themselves, the Immortals formed an elite military that swept over South Asia, North Africa, the Middle East, and Europe."
            },
            { "separator": true }
        ],
        "upgradesTo": "Pikeman",
        "obsoleteTech": "Civil Service",
        "hurryCostModifier": 19,
        "attackSound": "metalhit"
    },

    {
        "name": "Roman Legion",
        "replaces": "Swordsman",
        "uniqueTo": "Roma",
        "unitType": "Sword",
        "movement": 2,
        "strength": 14,
        "cost": 75,
        "requiredTech": "Iron Working",
        "promotions": ["[Roman Legion] ability"],
        "civilopediaText": [
            {
                "text": "The Roman legion was a unit of Roman troops formed for a particular war – until the Marian Reforms of 107 BCE when legions became virtually permanent. Legions included more than one kind of unit and were comprised of mounted soldiers and foot soldiers, generally on the order of a few thousand. Poorer spearmen (hastati) were positioned at the front of the line, while better-armed and armored soldiers (principes and triarii) were placed towards the rear. Soldiers were arranged in the maniple – an elaboration on the phalanx – which left empty spaces within the ranks to allow greater maneuverability and create deadly pockets where over-ambitious enemies might be trapped. These maniples were composed of around two hundred men and commanded by two centurions (each responsible for a hundred soldiers, as the name suggests). \nThe legions began as groups of nobles who could afford their own arms and equipment, but as Rome grew into an empire, these military units gave way to a professional (and political) class of soldiers, which had profound ramifications for the development of the Empire."
            },
            { "separator": true }
        ],
        "requiredResource": "Iron",
        "upgradesTo": "Longswordsman",
        "obsoleteTech": "Steel",
        "hurryCostModifier": 20,
        "attackSound": "metalhit"
    },

    {
        "name": "Hwarang",
        "replaces": "Composite Bowman",
        "uniqueTo": "Silla",
        "unitType": "Archery",
        "movement": 4,
        "strength": 7,
        "rangedStrength": 11,
        "cost": 75,
        "requiredTech": "Construction",
        "uniques": ["Can move after attacking", "No defensive terrain bonus"],
        "promotions": ["[Hwarang] ability"],
        "civilopediaText": [
            {
                "text": "The hwarang – a group of aristocratic youth bound together by a code of chivalry and emphasizing poetry and art as much as combat – have become a symbol of the romance and beauty of the Silla period well into the present day. A hwarang followed the sesok o-kye, the five commandments, emphasizing Confucian and Buddhist teachings, service to king and family, courage and loyalty to friends, and the wisdom of mercy. \nThe hwarang’s martial role diminished after the unification of Silla, and they largely disappeared from the historical record after Silla’s fall. They formally disbanded during Joseon."
            },
            { "separator": true }
        ],
        "obsoleteTech": "Machinery",
        "upgradesTo": "Crossbowman",
        "attackSound": "arrow"
    },

    {
        "name": "Tehina",
        "replaces": "Scout",
        "uniqueTo": "Tonga",
        "unitType": "Scout",
        "movement": 2,
        "strength": 5,
        "cost": 25,
        "uniques": [
            "Comment [May enter [Ocean] tiles after discovering [Optics]]",
            "May upgrade to [Archer] through ruins-like effects"
        ],
        "promotions": ["Ignore terrain cost", "[Tehina] ability"],
        "civilopediaText": [
            {
                "text": "Tongan society was a complex network of rank and status dependent on sibling and marriage relationships. Polygamy and divorce were common, so kinship lines could result in heavy competition for land and influence. A tehina, or 'younger brother', was often such a man, possibly an actual brother but perhaps a cousin or other close kinsman. The Tu'i Tonga would send these men out to other islands with the task to find a wife, marry into the local governance structure, and absorb the territory into the greater range of the Tu'i Tonga's rule. The chief would supply the tehina with the ships and warriors possibly needed for the expedition, but it was up to the tehina himself to acquire a wife of high rank and build a network of support in the new area. \nA successful tehina would wrap his new territory into the system of 'inasi, or 'tribute', that bound the Tongan maritime empire together. As a new de facto chief, his subjects were responsible for providing a portion of the land's product to him, and from him, on to the Tu'i Tonga in the center. In this way, the periphery continually expanded, and the network of kinship and exchange bound the center to the outward islands in what was a stable and lasting arrangement."
            },
            { "separator": true }
        ],
        "attackSound": "nonmetalhit"
    },

    {
        "name": "Kalia",
        "unitType": "Melee Water",
        "replaces": "Trireme",
        "uniqueTo": "Tonga",
        "movement": 4,
        "strength": 10,
        "cost": 45,
        "requiredTech": "Sailing",
        "uniques": ["Cannot enter ocean tiles"],
        "promotions": ["Coastal Raider I"],
        "civilopediaText": [
            {
                "text": "The kalia is a double-hulled canoe with large triangular sails similar to the drua of other Polynesian peoples. It developed from an earlier double-hulled vessel called the “tongiaki” which was similar to a catamaran. Kalia served two primary purposes in Tongan society – warfare and the transportation of goods. A kalia could hold dozens of men as well as carry food and prestige items from the periphery to the center, thus playing an important role in symbolizing the power of the king. Tongan navigators were a special class of people, valued for their expertise, and employed directly by the king or his chiefs. \nIt could take up to seven years to build a kalia, and the need for wood in their construction led Tongans to develop trade with the Fiji islands where lumber was more available. Tongan shipbuilders would establish outposts in Fiji, eventually developing into full settlements. Though it seems that Tongans originally acquired kalia by winning them from Fiji in warfare, Tongans came to hold a monopoly on their construction and use. No kalia would sail without a Tongan navigator and no resident of Fiji could visit Tonga except by kalia."
            },
            { "separator": true }
        ],
        "upgradesTo": "Caravel",
        "obsoleteTech": "Astronomy",
        "attackSound": "nonmetalhit"
    },

    {
        "name": "Mamluk",
        "replaces": "Knight",
        "uniqueTo": "The Abbasids",
        "unitType": "Mounted",
        "movement": 4,
        "strength": 18,
        "cost": 120,
        "requiredTech": "Chivalry",
        "obsoleteTech": "Military Science",
        "upgradesTo": "Cavalry",
        "requiredResource": "Horses",
        "uniques": [
            "[+25]% Strength <within [2] tiles of a [City center]>",
            "Can move after attacking",
            "No defensive terrain bonus",
            "[-33]% Strength <vs cities> <when attacking>"
        ],
        "civilopediaText": [
            {
                "text": "The Mamluk's rise to power is an epic tale. The word means “one who is owned.” In its historical context, it referred to Turkic (also Armenian, Sudanese, or others) slave-soldiers utilized by various Arab caliphates, beginning with the Abbasids. These men were often taken as children and raised as soldiers. Since these warriors lacked kin, or other social networks, that Arab soldiers often had, they could be deployed against other caliphates with impunity. \nWith military prowess and success came power and respect – even for someone enslaved. Despite their status as property, Mamluk standing rose above even the general freed population. They seized power in Egypt and Syria in 1250 CE, creating the Mamluk Sultanate that persisted for nearly three hundred years, and they decisively expelled the last Crusaders from the Holy Land."
            },
            { "separator": true }
        ],
        "attackSound": "horse"
    },

    {
        "name": "Bolyar",
        "replaces": "Knight",
        "uniqueTo": "Bulgaria",
        "unitType": "Mounted",
        "movement": 4,
        "strength": 20,
        "cost": 120,
        "requiredTech": "Chivalry",
        "obsoleteTech": "Military Science",
        "upgradesTo": "Cavalry",
        "requiredResource": "Horses",
        "uniques": [
            "Double movement in [Hill]",
            "Can move after attacking",
            "[-33]% Strength <vs cities> <when attacking>",
            "Never appears as a Barbarian unit"
        ],
        "civilopediaText": [
            {
                "text": "Known as a boila during the First Bulgarian Empire, a bolyar was an aristocrat of the Second Empire. These nobles held both civic and military roles, holding regional power during peacetime and responsible for activating during war. As highly ranked soldiers, they provided skills that made the Bulgarian army so effective – strong horsemanship and deep knowledge of the land. The cavalry was so valuable that neglect of a horse, even in peacetime, was harshly penalized. \nThe bolyar title was hereditary, and within their ranks there was an additional hierarchy, with a handful of greater bolyars serving as a royal council and the lesser bolyars focusing on regional governance."
            },
            { "separator": true }
        ],
        "promotions": ["Drill II"],
        "attackSound": "horse"
    },

    {
        "name": "Kalam",
        "replaces": "Caravel",
        "uniqueTo": "Chola",
        "unitType": "Melee Water",
        "movement": 4,
        "strength": 20,
        "cost": 120,
        "requiredTech": "Astronomy",
        "uniques": [
            "Can move after attacking",
            "[1] additional attacks per turn",
            "[+1] Sight",
            "Withdraws before melee combat <with [80]% chance>"
        ],
        "civilopediaText": [
            {
                "text": "By some definitions, the Chola navy was a vast enterprise, arranging conquests from Sri Lanka to Cambodia, extending trade routes from Africa to China, and amassing power over the Indian Ocean. However, by other definitions, the Chola navy did not even exist. Instead of a standing navy, in times of war, the Chola would contract a large number of merchant vessels and privateers (possibly pirates). The design of these ships and how they functioned is largely lost to history, and surviving records are riddled with inaccuracies. \nHere 'kalam' refers to 'vessel' in Tamil. It is not a terribly descriptive name, nor is it evocative, but it remains the most accurate term to describe a unit of the immense and diverse navy of the Cholas"
            },
            { "separator": true }
        ],
        "upgradesTo": "Ironclad",
        "obsoleteTech": "Steam Power",
        "attackSound": "nonmetalhit"
    },
    {
        "name": "Voi Chiến",
        "unitType": "Archery",
        "uniqueTo": "Đại Việt",
        "replaces": "Crossbowman",
        "movement": 3,
        "strength": 13,
        "rangedStrength": 23,
        "cost": 160,
        "requiredTech": "Machinery",
        "upgradesTo": "Gatling Gun",
        "obsoleteTech": "Industrialization",
        "uniques": [
            "[+1] Sight",
            "Can move after attacking",
            "No defensive terrain bonus"
        ],
        "civilopediaText": [
            {
                "text": "War elephants – voi chiến in Vietnamese – were a staple of armies across Southeast Asia. Able to move with surprising grace even when hauling heavy loads, they were vital in logistics, and in battle, they were terrifying. From a commanding height, mounted atop tons of hide and flesh, elephant-mounted warriors armed with spears, pikes, or bows could reach above and behind enemy lines, and the beast itself could be goaded into crashing through and trampling even seasoned soldiers. Cavalry, it seems, were particularly susceptible to an elephant charge, as horses required a great deal of discipline and training to keep up an assault when facing a mass of elephants. As such, they were the tanks of their day. \nBut an elephant is, after all, a living thing. In the wars against the Song Dynasty, the forces of Lý Thường Kiệt, emboldened by their success against the Cham to the south, deployed war elephants against the Chinese. However, Chinese forces had learned to attack the sensitive trunks of the animals, causing them to fly into a rage and trample their own forces \nElephants remained in use for their hauling capacity and relative stealth well into the Vietnam War."
            },
            { "separator": true }
        ],
        "attackSound": "crossbow"
    },

    {
        "name": "Leiomano",
        "replaces": "Swordsman",
        "uniqueTo": "Hawai'i",
        "unitType": "Sword",
        "movement": 2,
        "strength": 14,
        "cost": 75,
        "requiredTech": "Iron Working",
        "promotions": ["[Leiomano] ability"],
        "civilopediaText": [
            {
                "text": "How can weapons of war be fashioned without metal, obsidian, or flint? For the Hawaiians, the answer was found in the sea. The leiomano, the shark’s lei, is a broad wooden club rimmed with tiger shark teeth, which put the power of a shark's bite into the hands of individual Hawaiian warriors. \nHawai’i hasn't always been a unified archipelago. Some areas and islands had different kings, and these kings often engaged in bloody battles of conquest – until the rise of Kamehameha in the late 1700s. This leader engaged in a broad system of conquest to unite the fractious isles through outrigger, spear, and shark-tooth club."
            },
