            {
                "text": "The Ming Dynasty oversaw a revolution in battlefield tactics. Gunpowder, though long present in China, was being used for experimental weaponry, and Zhao Shi Zhen was a master experimenter. One of his creations was the 'quick thunder gun', or xunleichong: a machine gun, shield, and spear all in one. Five matchlock barrels protruded through a shield, providing the gunner some cover, while a detachable axe (for close-quarter fighting) served as a gun rest. When reversed, the apparatus could be used as a spear, thus the xunleichong was a ranged, melee, and anti-cavalry armament all at once. \nWas it practical? Was it ever deployed en masse? No, and no. But it remains a testament to experimentation and imagination, an attempt to provide new means to deal damage on the battlefield, taking the best advantage of new possibilities."
