        "civilopediaText": [
            {
                "text": "To get at the 'real' history of the shinobi (or ninja), we have to look at the peasant rebellions of the Sengoku era – the Ikko-Ikki and others. In the chaos of conflict, powerful non-noble families had to fend for themselves. Without the honor of aristocracy to uphold, non-nobles had a fair bit of leeway to use unconventional methods: impersonation, sabotage, infiltration, and the like. While the popular-culture image of a shinobi is of a masked assassin, most shinobi were not slinking around in black throwing shuriken at people – rather, they performed tasks such as posing as commoners or farmers to infiltrate a besieged fortress, or acting as enforcers or guards. In this way, a lot of ninja tools and implements look like farmer’s tools – the kama (sickle), for instance, rather than the sword. The iconic shuriken, of course, existed, and came in a number of uses and variations, from setting fires to planting caltrops to short-range thrown weapons. Of all of the clans of shinobi, it is the Iga, and the ninja retainer to Tokugawa, Hattori Hanzo, who has been best-remembered."
