        "civilopediaText": [
            {
                "text": "The samurai is the iconic symbol of the Sengoku period, both for us now as well as for the Japanese storytellers of the Edo period. Their reality was a little different. The term changed its meaning over time, from a noble knight before Sengoku, to a much broader definition during Sengoku, to a sort of scholar-bureaucrat during Edo. In Sengoku, a samurai was a person who attended to a daimyo, a lord. This could mean a wide variation in class and background, though in that time samurai were always those who served with arms, and were distinguished from the common ashigaru, the infantry. Samurai, for instance, could hold last names, and skill at battle could elevate someone to the samurai rank – and a skilled samurai could be elevated to a Sengoku daimyo. While samurai are most often thought of in association with the katana, the Japanese sword that marked a lighter and faster weapon than the earlier tachi, they could wield a variety of weapons and fought on horseback as well as on foot. Female samurai (onna-musha, technically of the warrior, or bushi, class) often preferred the naginata, the glaive."
