        "civilopediaText": [
            {
                "text": "Japan’s industrialization was meteoric. In 1867, Edo Japan was a prosperous but isolated society, shunning foreign influence in favor of its own order. In the following years, the Meiji regime launched an ambitious plan to shape Japan into one of the world’s leading industrial, and colonial, powers. Modernizing Japan’s military was an essential goal, and the development of warships that could compete with those of the West was key. \nDuring the Russo-Japanese War of 1904-5, a pre-dreadnaught battleship called the Mikasa was the flagship of the imperial fleet. She was constructed in the United Kingdom, housed nearly a thousand crew members, sported four 40-caliber twelve-inch guns, and featured an array of smaller armament. The Mikasa sank when her magazine exploded after the end of the conflict, but she was refloated the following year and continued to serve in subsequent wars until decommissioned in 1923."
            },
            { "separator": true }
        ],
        "attackSound": "shipguns"
    },

    {
        "name": "Soldaderas",
        "unitType": "Gunpowder",
        "uniqueTo": "Mexico",
        "replaces": "Rifleman",
        "strength": 34,
        "cost": 225,
        "movement": 2,
        "obsolete": "Replaceable Parts",
        "requiredTech": "Rifling",
        "upgradesTo": "Great War Infantry",
        "civilopediaText": [
            {
                "text": "The Mexican Revolution was a decade-long conflict between multiple power groups, beginning with the collapse of the dictatorship of Porfirio Díaz. In 1911, Díaz had jailed a prominent opponent, a wealthy landowner from the north of Mexico who had launched a rebellion against Díaz and his 'Porfiriato' – and won. This sparked a series of rebellions, reactions, coups, and attempted coups that eventually settled into a period of stability that has lasted from 1920 to the present day. \nFemale soldiers and army attendants, or soldaderas, played a key role in these conflicts. These varied across different armies and theaters of the revolution, from bands of female soldiers to camp followers to the wives and children of soldiers, thus increasing cohesion – and the humaneness – of soldiers in a chaotic battlefield. Famous soldaderas of the southern revolutionaries included Petra Herrera (a soldier in Pancho Villa’s army) and Ángela Jiménez (a master of demolitions)."
            },
            { "separator": true }
        ],
        "promotions": ["[Soldaderas] ability"] // +10 health for adjacent units
    },

    {
        "name": "Sepoy",
        "unitType": "Gunpowder",
        "uniqueTo": "The Mughals",
        "replaces": "Rifleman",
        "strength": 34,
        "cost": 225,
        "movement": 2,
        "requiredTech": "Rifling",
        "obsolete": "Plastics",
        "upgradesTo": "Great War Infantry",
        "civilopediaText": [
            {
                "text": "The name 'sepoy' initially referred to infantrymen in the Mughal Empire, armed with muskets and daggers, but through usage by the British East India Company, the term came to broadly refer to Indian soldiers under its employ. Sepoys were generally of low rank but were well-paid and trained in the arms they used. \nIn 1857, numerous sepoys (and other Indians) rose against the British East India Company. This rebellion came about as the Company sought to expand its ranks – local recruits vastly outnumbered the British, and the Company had largely recruited from upper-caste and well-remunerated locals (especially from Bengal). Integrating these units proved costly as higher-caste sepoys were granted separate dining facilities and exemption from fighting far from home. As the Company began to falter, it revoked many of these privileges and leaned on local troops. As missionaries became more and more visible, rumors abounded that the British were out to undermine Indian religion, both Hindu and Muslim. Finally, more rumors arose that the new Enfield rifle had charges laced with animal fat, which seemed to confirm the Company’s hostility to local beliefs. \nIn the wake of the rebellion, Britain took direct control over the Company’s possessions, citing mismanagement of public opinion as well as the exploitation of India for profit (but those practices didn't change much under the new regime)."
            },
            { "separator": true }
        ],
        "promotions": ["[Sepoy] ability", "Besiege"]
    },

    {
        "name": "Gurkha",
        "unitType": "Gunpowder",
        "uniqueTo": "Nepal",
        "replaces": "Infantry",
        "strength": 80,
        "cost": 400,
        "movement": 3,
        "uniques": [
            "Comment [Gain 10 Influence when gifting this unit to a City-State]"
        ],
        "civilopediaText": [
            {
                "text": "The name 'Gurkha' in its origin simply refers to the Gorkha Kingdom, one of the progenitors of the modern-day Nepalese state, but in the later 19th and early 20th centuries, the term has come to refer to Nepalese fighters contracted out to fight for other states. Most notably, this was the British – after the Anglo-Nepalese war, the British noted the loyalty and ferocity of Nepali fighters, and formed battalions of Gurkha who were willing to fight for the British. And fight they did – the Gurkhas fought for the British in the Indian Rebellion of 1857, World War I, and World War II. After Indian independence, Gurkhas continued to fight for the British, but many of them entered Indian service. \nThe most iconic symbol of the Gurkha is their kukri knife – a heavy, forward-curving blade that serves as a machete as well as a weapon."
            },
            { "separator": true }
        ],
        "upgradesTo": "Mechanized Infantry",
        "requiredTech": "Plastics",
        "obsolete": "Mobile Tactics"
    },

    {
        "name": "Prussian Hussar",
        "unitType": "Mounted",
        "uniqueTo": "Prussia",
        "replaces": "Cavalry",
        "movement": 5,
        "strength": 34,
        "cost": 225,
        "obsolete": "Mobile Tactics",
        "requiredTech": "Military Science",
        "upgradesTo": "Landship",
        "uniques": [
            "Can move after attacking",
            "No defensive terrain bonus",
            "[-33]% Strength <vs cities> <when attacking>"
        ],
        "civilopediaText": [
            {
                "text": "Friedrich the Great is famous for thinking holistically about battle. Rows of soldiers positioned to withstand enemy fire are well and good, but those warriors must first travel to the front lines and their morale depends on a full belly as much as an inspirational speech. As the Prussians understood, logistics are key to victory – and destroying the enemy’s plans is equally important. Hussars were lightly armored rapid cavalry bearing pistols, sabers, and lances, and they were masters of disrupting enemies through raids. \nAs befits their swashbuckling image, hussars were often dressed in elaborate uniforms. The Prussian hussars sported a skull and crossbones motif – an image that would later be re-used in the German empire to terrifying effect. Friedrich formed the first of these regiments, dressed all in black, and this uniform was in use throughout the Napoleonic Wars and into the trenches of World War I."
            },
            { "separator": true }
        ],
        "requiredResource": "Horses",
        "promotions": ["[Prussian Hussar] ability"]
    },

    {
        "name": "Stuka",
        "unitType": "Bomber",
        "uniqueTo": "Prussia",
        "replaces": "Bomber",
        "movement": 1,
        "strength": 75,
        "rangedStrength": 65,
        "range": 10,
        "cost": 375,
        "requiredTech": "Radar",
        "uniques": ["[+25]% Strength <vs [Land] units>"],
        "civilopediaText": [
            {
                "text": "The German Junkers Ju 87 or 'Stuka' was a ground-attack aircraft deployed in World War II, and was primarily used to disrupt shipping during the first few years of the conflict. The Stuka was designed to drop bombs as it dove toward the ground at an incredible velocity; it even included automatic recovery systems in case the pilot blacked out. Imagine the shock of a Stuka strike – its sirens blaring as it descends hundreds of feet in seconds, pulling away at the last minute. It would have been a terrifying strike for both pilot and target alike. \nThat steep, precipitous drop revealed a vulnerability within the Stuka design. Heavy bombers flew at a high altitude with a contingent of protective fighters; what they lacked in accuracy they made up for in relative pilot safety. However, hitting specific targets forced pilots to leave this formation and rendered them vulnerable to the increasingly sophisticated fighters of the era. By the end of the war, the era of dogfighting and dive-bombing was replaced by rockets and remote artillery – ominous signs for the ICBM arms race to come."
            },
            { "separator": true }
        ],
        "requiredResource": "Oil",
        "upgradesTo": "Stealth Bomber",
        "attackSound": "bombing"
    },

    {
        "name": "Gholām",
        "replaces": "Cavalry",
        "uniqueTo": "Qajar Iran",
        "unitType": "Mounted",
        "movement": 4,
        "strength": 34,
        "cost": 225,
        "requiredTech": "Military Science",
        "obsoleteTech": "Combustion",
        "requiredResource": "Horses",
        "upgradesTo": "Landship",
        "uniques": [
            "Can move after attacking",
            "No defensive terrain bonus",
            "[-33]% Strength <vs cities> <when attacking>",
            "Never appears as a Barbarian unit"
        ],
        "promotions": ["[Gholām] ability"],
        "civilopediaText": [
            {
                "text": "Emerging from the tradition of the Mamluks in the Abbasid Empire, gholāms were initially Turkish slave soldiers, first appearing in records during the Saffarid dynasty in the ninth century. Slavery was phased out under the Qajar centuries later, but gholāms remained a prominent, respected, and feared component of the 19th century Iranian army, particularly in their role as the royal bodyguard. They were divided into two units. The gholām-shāh were exclusively cavalrymen; they were the highest paid soldiers in the Qajar army, making more than six times more than the average soldier. The gholām-tofangchis ranked below the gholām-shāh, and fought on horseback and on foot wielding long rifles. \nThe gholāms remained Iran’s most elite soldiers until they were replaced by the Persian Cossack Brigade in 1879."
            },
            { "separator": true }
        ],
        "attackSound": "horse"
    },

    {
        "name": "Gusa",
        "unitType": "Gunpowder",
        "uniqueTo": "Qing",
        "replaces": "Rifleman",
        "strength": 34,
        "cost": 225,
        "movement": 2,
        "requiredTech": "Rifling",
        "upgradesTo": "Great War Infantry",
        "obsolete": "Replacable Parts",
        "civilopediaText": [
            {
                "text": "Numbers can be misleading. The Qing's so-called Eight Banners (or 'gusa') were actually twenty-four: eight for each of the Manchu, Han, and Mongol cohorts. Scholars estimate that a single gusa comprised about 18,000 troops and their families, but exact numbers remain unknown as records were kept hidden, likely for security reasons. \nThe gusa were more than military entities and represented an entire segment of Qing society, maintaining their own social and administrative customs. Membership in the gusa was hereditary, and service to one of the Eight Banners dictated one's ethnicity – for instance, a large group of Han bannermen transferred to the Manchu ranks became Manchu. \nThough modernization and westernization eventually rendered the gusa obsolete, for a time they were the might and pride of the Qing."
            },
            { "separator": true }
        ],
        "promotions": ["[Gusa] ability"]
    },

    {
        "name": "Katyusha",
        "replaces": "Artillery",
        "uniqueTo": "Imperial Russia",
        "unitType": "Siege",
        "movement": 4,
        "strength": 17,
        "rangedStrength": 21,
        "range": 3,
        "cost": 320,
        "requiredTech": "Dynamite",
        "upgradesTo": "Rocket Artillery",
        "obsoleteTech": "Rocketry",
        "uniques": [
            "[+200]% Strength <vs cities> <when attacking>",
            "[+100]% Strength <vs [Land] units> <when attacking>",
            "No defensive terrain bonus",
            "Must set up to ranged attack",
            "[-1] Sight"
        ],
        "promotions": ["Indirect Fire"],
        "civilopediaText": [
            {
                "text": "Long-range artillery has a few inherent problems. Over long distances, rockets run a very real chance of missing their target (without modern guidance systems). The Soviet solution to this problem was simple – why fire one rocket when you can fire 48? The Katyusha was a system of rails mounted on the back of a truck and loaded with rockets that could be fired in a single horrific salvo. While the Katyusha took more time to reload, its series of launchers could obliterate its target – and anything remotely close to it. \nGerman and Finnish troops referred to the Katyusha as 'Stalin’s organ', which wasn't a reference to the rocket’s shape, but to the howl the missiles made as they were fired, an unearthly, siren-like whistle that preceded a hellish bombardment. Katyusha systems remain in use in Africa and the Middle East."
            },
            { "separator": true }
        ],
        "attackSound": "missile"
    },

    {
        "name": "Chang Beun",
        "replaces": "Gatling Gun",
        "uniqueTo": "Rattanakosin",
        "unitType": "Ranged Gunpowder",
        "movement": 2,
        "strength": 30,
        "rangedStrength": 35,
        "range": 2,
        "cost": 225,
        "requiredTech": "Industrialization",
        "upgradesTo": "Machine Gun",
        "obsoleteTech": "Ballistics",
        "uniques": ["Can move after attacking"],
        "civilopediaText": [
            {
                "text": "During the medieval and early modern periods, a Siamese war elephant (a yuthahathi) was a royal steed, intended to carry knights and lords into pike duels with other kings. However, after the advent of guns, the elephant became a tank. Rather than using the archaic term 'Yuthahathi', we have translated 'Machine gun elephant' as simply 'Gun [carrying] elephant', or 'Chang Beun'. \nHowdah-mounted swivel guns, weighing 50-100kg, were a feature of the Thai/Burmese wars of the early modern era. Elephants retained some advantage over motorized tanks, as they could move more quietly through jungle and across rivers. These massive animals could also withstand bullets that horses and other living mounts could not. As such, they were deployed in conflicts that required mobility across difficult terrain, such as the Haw Wars (1865-1890)."
            },
            { "separator": true }
        ],
        "hurryCostModifier": 20,
        "attackSound": "elephant"
    },

